>Figure 2</label><caption><p>Effects of arsenic exposure (<italic>A; p</italic> = 0.001) and age (<italic>B; p</italic> = 0.486), sex (<italic>C; p</italic> = 0.0001), and smoking (<italic>D; p</italic> = 0.1018) on QTc interval using chi-square tests. The <italic>y</italic>-axis shows percent subjects with QTc interval &#x02265; 0.45 sec. The numbers above each bar are numerator for the number of subjects with QTc &#x02265; 0.45 sec and denominator for total number of subjects in this group.</p><p><italic>p</italic>-Values are from chi-square tests.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ehp0115-000690f2"/></fig><table-wrap id="t1-ehp0115-000690" position="float"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>Study subjects&#x02019; information, by low, medium, and high levels of arsenic.</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Characteristic</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Low (BDL&#x02013;21 &#x003bc;g/L)</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Medium (100&#x02013;300 &#x003bc;g/L)</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">High (430&#x02013;690 &#x003bc;g/L)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">No. of subjects</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">103</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">108</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">102</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Sex [no. (male/female)]</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">53/50</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">57/51</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">58/44</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Age [years (mean &#x000b1; SD)]</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">35 &#x000b1; 14</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">36 &#x000b1; 14</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">34 &#x000b1; 14</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Smoker (%)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" c